{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  // GPIO7 (BOOT button, see chipGpios below) is a genuine bidirectional
  // GPIO merely wired to a switch to GND with the RP2040's internal
  // pull-up enabled by software — not a dedicated/capacitive-only pin —
  // so it is handled entirely via the chipGpios "button" tag (full
  // capability set retained), matching the adafruit_feather_scorpio and
  // adafruit_feather_can precedents. The six EPD (e-Ink display
  // connector) control lines and the NeoPixel power-enable line are
  // likewise genuine bidirectional GPIOs merely wired to dedicated
  // onboard roles, so they too are handled via chipGpios (emptied
  // capabilities), not inputOnlyGpios.
  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This is the plain (non-WiFi)
  // "rp2040" chip bucket; the board's EPD support circuitry is an SPI-
  // attached e-Ink/e-Paper display connector, not a pfodWeb "connections"
  // transport in its own right.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (RP2040/RP2350 family button/LED/NeoPixel + dedicated-
  // hardware pin-exclusion audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: Adafruit "Feather RP2040 ThinkInk" (product 5727) — a Feather
  // RP2040 with dedicated support circuitry (six GPIOs wired to a 24-pin
  // FPC connector, per Adafruit: "No display is included onboard...
  // support circuitry for common 24-pin e-Paper/e-Ink displays") for
  // driving an externally-attached e-Ink/e-Paper display over its own
  // dedicated SPI bus, plus a plain status LED (D13), an onboard single
  // status NeoPixel (GPIO21, with a separate power-enable gate on
  // GPIO20), and a BOOT button (GPIO7) — the same identity-pin pattern
  // as the already-audited adafruit_feather_scorpio and
  // adafruit_feather_can sibling Feather RP2040 boards.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  // 1. learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-rp2040-feather-thinkink/pinouts
  //    (official Adafruit guide) — quotes verbatim:
  //      "Above the USB C connector is the D13 LED. This little red LED
  //      is controllable in CircuitPython code using board.LED, and in
  //      Arduino as PIN_LED."
  //      "Above the pin labels for D24 and D25 is the status NeoPixel
  //      LED. It is connected to GPIO21."
  //      "Additionally, there is a NeoPixel power pin on GPIO20. It
  //      needs to be set High for the NeoPixel to be powered."
  //      "The Boot button is the button on the right, located on GPIO7.
  //      It is available as board.BUTTON in CircuitPython, and is
  //      available for use in Arduino as PIN_BUTTON."
  //      "EPD_BUSY/GPIO16 - The Busy Detect pin for e-Ink displays. It
  //      is also SPI0 MISO, UART0 TX, I2C0 SDA and PWM0 A."
  //      "EPD_RESET/GPIO17 - The Reset pin for e-Ink displays. It is
  //      also SPI0 CS, UART0 RX, I2C0 SCL and PWM0 B."
  //      "EPD_DC/GPIO18 - The Data Command pin for e-Ink displays. It
  //      is also SPI0 SCK, I2C1 SDA and PWM1 A."
  //      "EPD_CS/GPIO19 - The Chip Select pin for e-Ink displays. It is
  //      also SPI0 MOSI, I2C1 SCL and PWM1 B."
  //      "EPD_SCK/GPIO22 - The SPI SCK pin for e-Ink displays. It is
  //      also SPI0 SCK, I2C1 SDA and PWM3 A."
  //      "EPD_MOSI/GPIO23 - The SPI MOSI pin for e-Ink displays. It is
  //      also SPI0 MOSI, I2C1 SCL and PWM3 B."
  //    (The "SPI0"/"SPI1" bus names in Adafruit's per-pin quotes above
  //    refer to the RP2040's raw hardware SPI0/SPI1 peripherals, which
  //    this board's pins_arduino.h maps the opposite way round via its
  //    __SPI0_DEVICE/__SPI1_DEVICE indirection — the Arduino-facing
  //    "SPI1" object (__SPI1_DEVICE spi0) is the EPD bus: PIN_SPI1_SS
  //    (19u) / PIN_SPI1_SCK (22u) / PIN_SPI1_MOSI (23u) match EPD_CS/
  //    EPD_SCK/EPD_MOSI exactly, and PIN_SPI1_MISO is left "not pinned
  //    out" (31u) — consistent with an e-Ink display, which is
  //    write-only over SPI and needs no MISO line.)
  //    Also separately confirms GPIO8/13/14/15 (the Arduino-facing
  //    "SPI0" object, __SPI0_DEVICE spi1) are the board's general-
  //    purpose exposed Feather header SPI pins: "GPIO8 (MI): The main
  //    SPI1 MISO...", "GPIO13 (D13): Digital I/O pin 13. It is also
  //    SPI1 CS...", "GPIO14 (SCK): The main SPI1 SCK...", "GPIO15 (MO):
  //    The main SPI1 MOSI..." — i.e. GPIO13 is merely the vendor's
  //    default SS pin for this shared/general-purpose bus, not consumed
  //    by the EPD or any other onboard peripheral (see "Bug found"
  //    below).
  // 2. learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-rp2040-feather-thinkink (Overview,
  //    view=all) — official Adafruit guide, confirms: "No display is
  //    included onboard. The board features support circuitry for
  //    common 24-pin e-Paper/e-Ink displays" via a 24-pin FPC connector;
  //    "One red LED on Pin #13 for general-purpose use"; "RGB NeoPixel
  //    for full-color indication"; a separate "Bootloader select
  //    button" alongside a Reset button.
  // 3. raw.githubusercontent.com/adafruit/circuitpython (main)
  //    ports/raspberrypi/boards/adafruit_feather_rp2040_thinkink/
  //    mpconfigboard.h and pins.c — official CircuitPython board port,
  //    independently cross-referencing the same hardware: confirms
  //    CIRCUITPY_STATUS_LED_POWER -> GPIO20, MICROPY_HW_NEOPIXEL ->
  //    GPIO21, and pins.c maps LED->GPIO13, BUTTON/BOOT->GPIO7,
  //    NEOPIXEL->GPIO21, NEOPIXEL_POWER->GPIO20, EPD_BUSY->GPIO16,
  //    EPD_RESET->GPIO17, EPD_DC->GPIO18, EPD_CS->GPIO19, EPD_SCK->
  //    GPIO22, EPD_MOSI->GPIO23 — an exact match to this board's own
  //    pins_arduino.h aliases.
  // 4. raw.githubusercontent.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr (main)
  //    boards/adafruit/feather_rp2040/adafruit_feather_rp2040.dts — no
  //    Zephyr board port exists for this specific ThinkInk variant, but
  //    the plain sibling "Feather RP2040" (same D13-LED / GPIO7-BOOT-
  //    button module design, reused verbatim across this entire
  //    Adafruit Feather RP2040 product line per source 1's identical
  //    phrasing) confirms: leds { led0: led_0 { gpios = <&gpio0 13
  //    GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; }; } — i.e. D13 LED is active-high, matching
  //    the adafruit_feather_scorpio/adafruit_feather_can precedents'
  //    independently-confirmed active-high D13 LED on the same module.
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/adafruit_feather_thinkink/notes.txt
  "chipGpios": {
    "7":  { "capabilities": ["digital_input", "digital_output", "pwm_output", "button"], "note": "PIN_BUTTON/BOOT — bootloader-select pushbutton, active-low" },
    "13": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED — onboard status LED, active-high" },
    "16": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_BUSY — Busy-Detect control line for this board's e-Ink" },
    "17": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_RESET — active-reset control line for this board's e-Ink" },
    "18": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_DC — Data/Command select line for this board's e-Ink" },
    "19": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_CS — dedicated SPI chip-select line for this board's e-Ink" },
    "20": { "capabilities": [], "note": "NEOPIXEL_POWER — drive HIGH to power NeoPixel on GPIO21" },
    "21": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel"], "note": "PIN_NEOPIXEL — needs GPIO20 driven HIGH to light" },
    "22": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_SCK — dedicated SPI clock line for this board's e-Ink" },
    "23": { "capabilities": [], "note": "EPD_MOSI — dedicated SPI MOSI line for this board's e-Ink" }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. This board's pins_arduino.h declares no analog
  // aliases of its own (its bespoke D0-D29/A0-A3 static-const block
  // mirrors the chip's standard A0-A3 = GPIO26-29 mapping), and this
  // audit found no reason to override that.
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
